"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,229\nThe Learning Brain. Series 3: Generative AI\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,435\nEpisode 12\n\n3\n00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,621\nDesigning for both\n\n4\n00:00:12,421 --> 00:00:15,411\nAcross eleven episodes, one pattern repeats.\n\n5\n00:00:15,782 --> 00:00:17,395\nWhen AI does the thinking,\n\n6\n00:00:17,444 --> 00:00:20,194\nperformance can rise while learning falls.\n\n7\n00:00:20,582 --> 00:00:22,470\nWhen AI makes students think,\n\n8\n00:00:22,566 --> 00:00:23,849\nboth can rise.\n\n9\n00:00:24,168 --> 00:00:25,996\nWe saw it in maths classrooms,\n\n10\n00:00:26,016 --> 00:00:28,235\nphysics courses, consulting firms,\n\n11\n00:00:28,255 --> 00:00:29,544\nand essay writing.\n\n12\n00:00:29,714 --> 00:00:30,613\nHere is the map.\n\n13\n00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:32,697\nPrediction, in episode one.\n\n14\n00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:34,083\nOffloading, two.\n\n15\n00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,901\nThe jagged frontier, three.\n\n16\n00:00:36,012 --> 00:00:37,609\nTutors that harm or help,\n\n17\n00:00:37,629 --> 00:00:38,690\nfour and five.\n\n18\n00:00:38,939 --> 00:00:40,301\nCalibration, six.\n\n19\n00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:42,299\nAutomation bias, seven.\n\n20\n00:00:42,369 --> 00:00:44,353\nCritical thinking, eight.\n\n21\n00:00:44,373 --> 00:00:45,637\nWriting, nine.\n\n22\n00:00:45,657 --> 00:00:46,632\nCreativity, ten.\n\n23\n00:00:46,652 --> 00:00:48,387\nAnd verification, eleven.\n\n24\n00:00:48,557 --> 00:00:50,628\nBefore AI, students commit.\n\n25\n00:00:51,092 --> 00:00:54,587\nThey predict, draft, and generate ideas on their own,\n\n26\n00:00:54,761 --> 00:00:56,586\nso there is something to learn from,\n\n27\n00:00:56,606 --> 00:00:58,415\nand something to compare against.\n\n28\n00:00:58,585 --> 00:01:02,045\nDuring AI, the tool should hint rather than solve,\n\n29\n00:01:02,267 --> 00:01:03,584\nask before it tells,\n\n30\n00:01:03,604 --> 00:01:05,607\nand run on correct material.\n\n31\n00:01:06,069 --> 00:01:08,121\nStudents explain its reasoning back,\n\n32\n00:01:08,141 --> 00:01:09,502\nand look for a flaw.\n\n33\n00:01:09,672 --> 00:01:12,923\nAfter AI, students verify sources,\n\n34\n00:01:13,124 --> 00:01:15,419\ncheck their confidence against results,\n\n35\n00:01:15,657 --> 00:01:17,843\nand do some unassisted practice,\n\n36\n00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:20,763\nso everyone can see what they can do alone.\n\n37\n00:01:20,933 --> 00:01:24,442\nThe evidence for this pattern is strongest where it is oldest: retrieval,\n\n38\n00:01:24,753 --> 00:01:27,625\ngeneration, and metacognition.\n\n39\n00:01:28,128 --> 00:01:30,800\nThe AI studies are newer and smaller,\n\n40\n00:01:31,032 --> 00:01:34,883\nso treat each one as a hypothesis to test in your own course.\n\n41\n00:01:35,307 --> 00:01:38,320\nRun small comparisons, and share what you find.\n\n42\n00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:39,510\nFor your next course,\n\n43\n00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:40,653\npick one assignment.\n\n44\n00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:43,458\nAdd a commit step before AI,\n\n45\n00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:46,757\na hint only tutor or a critique step during it,\n\n46\n00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:48,709\nand a verify step after.\n\n47\n00:01:49,162 --> 00:01:52,460\nThen compare unassisted performance with last year.\n\n48\n00:01:52,630 --> 00:01:55,021\nAI and the brain are not rivals.\n\n49\n00:01:55,350 --> 00:01:58,322\nDesigned well, each makes the other stronger.\n\n"
